---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';

const path = '/use-cases/welcome-series';
const title = 'Shopify welcome series: entry, offers and first-purchase exits';
const desc = 'Design a welcome series around signup purpose, channel permission, offer eligibility, purchase exits and incremental contribution.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Welcome series</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Welcome the subscriber you actually have</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A new signup is a request with a source and a purpose. Build the welcome journey around that request, then stop first-purchase persuasion when the customer buys. A new address in a database is not enough to decide what to send.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore app options</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/list-growth">Review capture promises</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Define entry before writing the sequence</h2>
      <p>Keep the capture source, signup time, identity used, stated purpose, disclosure version and channel permission evidence together. Distinguish a newsletter request, a first-order offer, an event registration and a product waitlist. Route an imported record with missing evidence to review; do not treat the import date as a fresh signup.</p>
      <p>Resolve whether the person is new to the store or merely new to a channel. Check purchase history against a reliable customer identity before granting a new-customer incentive. If two records may belong to different people, hold personalized offers rather than merging on a shared surname, address or phone number alone. A gift recipient is not automatically the purchaser or a marketing subscriber.</p>
      <p>Choose an explicit reentry policy: what constitutes a new request, which earlier enrollment it supersedes, and whether a previously redeemed benefit remains unavailable. Resubscribing does not erase purchase or redemption history. Preserve withdrawal evidence and require a new valid permission record for any resumed marketing.</p>
      <h3>Confirmation has its own job</h3>
      <p>If the capture process requires confirmation, keep that request separate from promotional enrollment. A pending confirmation should not silently start an offer sequence. A confirmation message should explain the action being confirmed; do not use it as a container for unrelated marketing. Resolve permission separately for email and SMS, for the relevant purpose and sender. Permission for one channel does not establish permission for another.</p>
      <p>Record the approved sending window and the evidence behind it. If the relevant permission, identity or local sending-time decision is unresolved, hold the marketing job. The <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours guide</a> provide questions to take into that review; a workflow setting alone is not a legal assurance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Give each message a reason to exist</h2>
      <p>Start with the capture promise: deliver the requested resource or explain an eligible offer and its actual terms. Product education can follow when it answers a plausible first-purchase question, such as sizing, compatibility, ingredients or returns. Let the subscriber choose interests where useful instead of inferring a personal need from a gift order.</p>
      <p>Use email when the permitted message needs space for comparison or instructions. Consider an SMS step only when its own permission and a concise, timely purpose justify the interruption. Do not reproduce every email as a text. Choose delays and a journey expiry from the buying decision and the signup promise; there is no universal number of steps or waiting period.</p>
      <p>A store selling equipment may test a compatibility explanation before an offer reminder. A gift-led store may ask whether the shopper wants gifting ideas or products for themselves. These are possible content choices, not observed performance results. If a step adds no new decision help, remove it.</p>
      <h3>Make the offer enforceable</h3>
      <p>Store an offer version with eligibility, product exclusions, minimum spend, combination rules, redemption limit, currency and an unambiguous expiry with timezone. Check that the checkout can enforce those terms. Do not describe a code as personal or single-use unless its implementation supports that promise.</p>
      <p>Separate permission to receive marketing from eligibility for a discount. An existing buyer can request useful updates while being ineligible for a first-order offer. Explain that distinction at capture. If the advertised benefit cannot be honored, pause the affected entry path and resolve the promise; quietly replacing it with a weaker offer creates a different expectation.</p>
      <p>When terms change, decide how outstanding promises will be fulfilled and retire superseded queued copy. Recheck redemption, eligibility and expiry at dispatch and at checkout. A reminder queued before expiry must not imply that an expired offer is still available, and a refreshed signup must not restart its countdown.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Exit and arbitration rules come before extra touches</h2>
      <p>A confirmed first purchase retires outstanding first-purchase persuasion across channels. Hand off to <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase guidance</a> only when the new message has its own appropriate purpose and permission. A cancellation or refund should not automatically reissue the acquisition incentive; decide whether the person needs support or a separately reviewed journey.</p>
      <p>Hold promotional welcome steps while an unresolved support issue makes them inappropriate. On withdrawal, cancel queued marketing covered by that withdrawal and update downstream senders. Preserve independently permitted channels only after resolving the scope; do not automatically equate an SMS withdrawal with an email request. Unknown scope requires review before more marketing.</p>
      <p>Assign one owner for cross-journey decisions. A cart message, a launch and a welcome reminder should compete for a shared contact budget, not each assume they own an empty calendar. Protect a genuinely promised delivery of a benefit while suppressing redundant reminders. For a subscriber with an active cart, decide whether <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart guidance</a> replaces the welcome nudge and which offer remains valid. Record the winning reason and cancel or expire the losing job rather than accumulating postponed promotions.</p>
      <p>Evaluate channel and combined contact limits against recent actual sends, including broadcasts. Recheck support, purchase, permission, offer version and competing journeys immediately before dispatch. Configure an expiry for any delayed message so that a once-relevant welcome does not emerge weeks later.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Recover without restarting the welcome</h2>
      <p>Use a stable signup event identifier and enrollment identifier to deduplicate repeated deliveries. A duplicate signup should resolve to the same enrollment, not issue another code or restart all steps. Keep event time distinct from arrival time: a late purchase can invalidate a job even if the signup event arrived first.</p>
      <p>Version the journey and stamp queued jobs with enrollment, step, offer version and expiry. On a change or identity merge, reconcile old enrollments and retire stale jobs. Keep the audit record of why a step was sent, skipped or cancelled without copying unnecessary personal data into logs.</p>
      <p>After an outage, reconcile purchase and withdrawal events before releasing any backlog. Re-evaluate each unsent step against current state; skip expired messages and avoid a burst of accumulated steps. If the sender times out after accepting a request, check its delivery record or supported deduplication mechanism before retrying. An uncertain delivery is not proof that nothing was sent. If suppression state cannot be trusted, hold marketing until reconciliation finishes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>Every scenario below is hypothetical: a proposed acceptance fixture for a welcome design, not a merchant case study or a reported result.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Newsletter email only:</strong> a person requests email updates and supplies no SMS permission. Expected: eligible email enrollment only; no text is scheduled.</li>
        <li><strong>Confirmation pending:</strong> a signup awaits the required confirmation. Expected: the confirmation request can proceed under the approved process; promotional steps remain blocked until the required state is reached.</li>
        <li><strong>Existing buyer and repeat signup:</strong> a buyer submits the first-order form again after using its benefit. Expected: no new first-order code, no duplicate enrollment; explain eligible alternatives without erasing redemption history.</li>
        <li><strong>Gift and shared identity:</strong> an order contains a gift recipient and a phone shared by a household. Expected: no inferred recipient marketing permission, no automatic identity merge and no personalized purchase claim until identity is resolved.</li>
        <li><strong>Offer version changes:</strong> a reminder for an older offer waits behind a sending window. Expected: honor or resolve the original promise under the chosen policy; retire incompatible copy and never silently extend expiry.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchase, support or withdrawal:</strong> each event occurs while a promotional job is queued. Expected: purchase ends acquisition persuasion; an unresolved support issue holds it; withdrawal cancels marketing within its scope. Test these as separate states.</li>
        <li><strong>Cart collision:</strong> a cart nudge and welcome offer target the same person. Expected: the documented owner selects one useful message and one consistent offer; the losing job cannot reappear after its useful window.</li>
        <li><strong>Duplicate, late event and outage:</strong> replay the signup twice, deliver an earlier purchase late, and restore a stale reminder after an outage. Expected: one enrollment, no acquisition reminder to the buyer and no backlog burst. An ambiguous sender timeout is reconciled before retry.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Measure contribution from assigned subscribers</h2>
      <p>Compare the complete welcome treatment with a randomized holdout among equally eligible new subscribers. Keep assignment stable across identity resolution and channels. Preserve promised benefits and necessary confirmation or service messages in both groups; define precisely which optional promotional steps differ. If testing a richer sequence against a basic welcome, the result measures that difference, not the value of all welcome activity.</p>
      <p>Choose the observation window before launch so both groups have comparable time to purchase and return products. Report first-purchase rate, net contribution per assigned subscriber, withdrawals, complaints and support load. Include skipped sends and non-openers in their assigned groups. Report missing identity or late-event coverage and uncertainty; a small difference is not a reliable promise of lift.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical arithmetic: with 1,000 assigned subscribers in each group, treatment contribution of $4,600 and holdout contribution of $4,200 gives a $400 difference. If those figures already subtract product costs, discounts, fulfillment and refunds, do not subtract those costs again. After an additional $150 in messaging and $50 in support costs, the remaining difference is $200, or $0.20 per assigned treatment subscriber. Include other incremental program costs if not already counted. These illustrative values are not a forecast.</p>
      <p>Platform-attributed orders can help trace activity but cannot alone establish incremental effect. Track offer leakage, repeat redemptions and purchase exits as operating signals alongside the experiment. Review <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">measurement design</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a> before expanding an incentive.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Choose software against the welcome contract</h2>
      <p>A shared email and SMS workspace can fit if it preserves separate channel permission and resolves collisions. Separate senders can fit if the team can synchronize exits and inspect queued jobs across them. A simple email welcome can fit when the request and content are email-led. Select a setup whose actual behavior matches your entry, offer and recovery rules.</p>
      <p>Ask shortlisted providers to demonstrate pending confirmation, existing-buyer exclusion, offer-version retirement, purchase and withdrawal races, and ambiguous retry handling using your proposed fixtures. Verify current capabilities, limits and costs with the provider before committing. Use the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">cost worksheet</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination guide</a> to frame that evaluation.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
